## Problem
Building XPath expressions via string concatenation lets attackers inject XPath syntax (e.g., `' or '1'='1`) to bypass authentication or extract data.

## Solutions

### Option 1: Use XPath Variables (Best — true parameterization)

```java
import javax.xml.xpath.*;
import javax.xml.namespace.QName;

XPath xpath = XPathFactory.newInstance().newXPath();

// Set variable resolver
xpath.setXPathVariableResolver(variableName -> {
    switch (variableName.getLocalPart()) {
        case "userVar": return user;
        case "passVar": return pass;
        default: return null;
    }
});

String expression = "/users/user[@name=$userVar and @pass=$passVar]";
XPathExpression expr = xpath.compile(expression);
Object result = expr.evaluate(document, XPathConstants.NODESET);
```

This never mixes user input into the expression string — the XPath engine treats the values as data, not code.

### Option 2: Sanitize/Escape Input (if variables aren't available)

If you must concatenate (e.g., legacy code, older parser without variable support), strictly validate and escape:

```java
public static String escapeXPathLiteral(String input) {
    if (input == null) return "";
    if (!input.contains("'")) {
        return "'" + input + "'";
    }
    if (!input.contains("\"")) {
        return "\"" + input + "\"";
    }
    // Contains both quote types — use concat()
    StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder("concat(");
    String[] parts = input.split("'");
    for (int i = 0; i < parts.length; i++) {
        sb.append("'").append(parts[i]).append("'");
        if (i < parts.length - 1) sb.append(",\"'\",");
    }
    sb.append(")");
    return sb.toString();
}

String expression = "/users/user[@name=" + escapeXPathLiteral(user) +
                     " and @pass=" + escapeXPathLiteral(pass) + "]";
```

### Option 3: Strong Input Validation (defense-in-depth)

Combine with allowlist validation regardless of method chosen:

```java
private static final Pattern SAFE_INPUT = Pattern.compile("^[a-zA-Z0-9_.@-]{1,64}$");

public static void validate(String input) {
    if (input == null || !SAFE_INPUT.matcher(input).matches()) {
        throw new IllegalArgumentException("Invalid input");
    }
}

validate(user);
validate(pass);
```

## Recommendation

1. **Use Option 1 (XPath variables)** — it's the direct equivalent of prepared statements/parameterized queries for XPath and fully eliminates injection.
2. Add **input validation** (Option 3) as defense-in-depth, especially for usernames.
3. **Never store or compare plaintext passwords** in XML — hash passwords before comparison, ideally doing the password check in application code after retrieving the user record by name only:

```java
String expression = "/users/user[@name=$userVar]";
// ... fetch node, then verify password hash in Java using BCrypt/Argon2, not XPath
```

This also avoids leaking timing information about password matches and keeps credential verification logic in your application, not the XML query.

